WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.320
In this video, you protect a virtual machine with harvey, a command-line disaster recovery tool, and fail it over to a second SUSE Virtualization cluster.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:15.920
On the retail-store cluster, the virtual machine demo-harvey is running. Its disk is a Longhorn volume.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:25.300
In a terminal, run harvey protect for demo-harvey. harvey runs on your workstation and talks to both clusters.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:31.290
harvey takes a first backup to a shared S3 target and waits until the disaster-recovery cluster restores it.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:37.920
The source volume now backs up every five minutes. On the other cluster, a standby DR volume keeps restoring each backup.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.540
harvey also copies the virtual machine and its cloud-init secret. The replica is created stopped.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:49.730
On the disaster-recovery cluster, filter the virtual machine list for demo-harvey.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:55.070
The replica is there, and Off. It uses no compute until you fail over.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:02.580
harvey replication status shows the DR volume in standby and up to date. Lag is the data you would lose right now.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:08.250
Now run a planned failover of demo-harvey to the disaster-recovery cluster.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:14.710
harvey stops the VM, takes a final backup, waits for its restore, then promotes the DR volume and starts the VM.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:21.850
The summary shows the total recovery time, split by phase, and a recovery point of zero seconds: no data lost.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:26.250
Back on the disaster-recovery cluster, filter for demo-harvey again.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:31.190
The virtual machine is Running here now, on the restored Longhorn volume.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:39.530
On retail-store, the original is Off, so only one copy runs. harvey reprotect later replicates back the other way.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:48.085
demo-harvey now runs on the disaster-recovery cluster, with no data lost. Next, you protect virtual machines with CloudCasa.
